<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="lab15">
  <title>Lab 15</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      This lab is intended to practice ideas related to solving partial differential equations. By the end, you'll have practiced
      <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Using the separation of variables method to reduce the PDE to ODEs,
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solving ODE boundary value problems, and
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
              Determining Fourier coefficients to satisfy an initial condition. 
            </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      In the first exercise, you'll also be able to visualize your final solution to verify it is reasonable.
    </p>
    <p>This is an opportunity
      to explore important concepts with others, explain your understanding, and ask questions. As such, these exercises should
      be thought of as a starting point for exploration, and you should feel free to ask related questions or explore related ideas
      if your interest is led somewhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will be graded on effort and engagement during class time, not necessarily on completing every exercise. Guidelines
      for working together may be provided in class.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
<page>
  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
  <title>Heat Equation</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
			Follow the steps below to solve <m>u_t = u_{xx}</m> for <m>x \in [0,1]</m>, <m>t \gt 0</m>, and
                    <md>
                        u_x(0,t) = u_x(1,t)= 0
                    </md>,
                    <md>
                        u(x,0) =  \begin{cases}
                                    x \amp \text{if } x \in [0,0.5] \\
                                    x - 0.5 \amp \text{if } x \in (0.5, 1]
                                    \end{cases}
                    </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
        Use separation of variables to get two ODEs. 
               
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
        Recognize the time ODE is separable, and write a general solution for it.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
             Use the boundary conditions for the PDE to establish boundary conditions
             for the space ODE. Then find all solutions to the space ODE. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Identify the infinitely many product solutions we now have. Use superposition to 
                write the general solution (it will be a series solution).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the initial condition to determine the Fourier coefficients in your general solution (do not simplify them).
                It will be useful to reference the <url href="https://jshriner6.github.io/math271-FA2026/course/orthogonal-functions-complete.html">important exmaples of orthogonal sets</url>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Enter your Fourier series solution into <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d">Desmos</url> and verify your solution is reasonable.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    
    
</exercise>



</page>

<page>
    <exercise>
  <title>Schrödinger's Equation</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
			Follow the steps below to solve <m>i\overline{h}\psi_t = -\frac{\overline{h}^2}{2}\psi_{xx}</m> for <m>x \in [0,2]</m>, <m>t \gt 0</m>, and
                    <md>
                        \psi(0) = \psi(2)= 0
                    </md>,
                    <md>
                        \psi(x,0)= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
                    </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
        Use separation of variables to get two ODEs. 
               
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
        Recognize the time ODE is separable, and write a general solution for it.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
             Use the boundary conditions for the PDE to establish boundary conditions
             for the space ODE. Then find all (normalized) solutions to the space ODE. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Identify the infinitely many product solutions we now have. Use superposition to 
                write the general solution (it will be a series solution).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the initial condition to determine the Fourier coefficients in your general solution (do not simplify them).
                It will be useful to reference the <url href="https://jshriner6.github.io/math271-FA2026/course/orthogonal-functions-complete.html">important exmaples of orthogonal sets</url>.
                Then 
                write your particular solution as a series solution.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    
    
</exercise>
</page>


</worksheet>
